statements.text:
The three documents hold the source-required content as checkable text.

specification/project-conformance.md contains:
- the sentence declaring the project pressure for a JavaScript Hello World software build traversal;
- the seven downstream surfaces, listed under their source names;
- the ABG-ownership sentence verbatim, followed by a note that it describes the building environment, grants no authority and places no runtime in the subject;
- a statement that it is the authority for design/implementation-design.md.

design/implementation-design.md contains:
- the line 'Authority: specification/project-conformance.md';
- the minimal helloWorld module and its exact return value;
- the package fields;
- package.json, src/hello.mjs, design/test-design.md, both verifier paths, test-execution-plan.json and 'execution result', each named as a separate surface; the execution result is marked as having no file.

design/test-design.md contains:
- 'Evidence source: package.json and src/hello.mjs';
- the component obligation: import helloWorld and assert strict equality with "Hello, world!";
- the UAT obligation: check the user-visible greeting contract through the same exported function.

None of the documents contains code, a plan or a claimed result. check-documents proves only that these elements are present. Semantic assessment still has to judge faithfulness and sufficiency (req-pressure:13).

test-execution-plan.json declares:
- command "node";
- args ["--test", "test/component/hello.test.mjs", "test/uat/hello.uat.test.mjs"];
- expectedTestPassCount 2;
- expectedStdoutMatch "fail 0";
- assertedReturnValue "Hello, world!";
- derivedFrom, listing the two verifier paths.

The plan has no observed-result field. "fail 0" appears in both Node reporter summaries ('ℹ fail 0' in spec, '# fail 0' in TAP). It is not yet observed which of the two Node 24.7.0 uses under capture. No depth coverage is added, so the source would allow pinning a pass count; the design does not pin one, as a conservative choice (req-pressure:22). Writing the plan executes nothing. The design declares the planned command literally, and check-plan proves it equals the plan. The command is not read from the plan at run time.

The acceptance conditions map onto observations of planned-verifier-run as follows.
- Component passed: that run's admitted stdout shows a passing 'component:' test, corroborated by component-suite-run's exit and pass predicates.
- UAT passed: the same check for 'uat:'.
- observedTestPassCount >= expectedTestPassCount: the test_pass_count predicate at the plan value 2.
- planSatisfied: all of the following hold:
  - the run's command and args equal the plan (check-plan);
  - the pass floor holds;
  - the exit status is 0;
  - expectedStdoutMatch occurs in that run's admitted stdout;
  - assertedReturnValue equals the declared expectedReturnValue (check-plan);
  - the typed observation matches.

A differing reporter glyph fails no condition, and no failure is excused. No declared predicate matches a substring of stdout, so the Evidence stage must compare the fragment against the admitted record (design-pressure:02). This definition of planSatisfied is a proposal (req-pressure:04). Acceptance itself remains an owner ruling.

Each artifact records where it was derived from:
- the implementation design cites the conformance document as its authority;
- src/hello.mjs names the implementation design;
- the test design names package.json and src/hello.mjs as its evidence source;
- the component verifier names the test design and the source;
- the UAT verifier names the component verifier, the test design and the source;
- the plan's derivedFrom lists both verifiers.

The constructor writes the surfaces in that source order. check-derivation proves that each edge is named, and that function name, export form, module path, return value, verifier paths and command all agree. All targets are built in one construction activation. A predecessor built in the same activation cannot enter a dependent's read paths declared before construction, so construction order cannot be observed independently. The digest-basis clause of requirement-candidate:13 therefore remains an explicit gap (design-pressure:01).

The design does not reproduce the source's stage vector: the per-turn 'Write only' limits, the 'in this vector' prohibitions, the no-execution limit on the plan stage, executeFromPlan: false, and the witness parityRule that asks for the odd_sdlc traversal shape as typed GTL vectors. It uses one native construction of all targets, then admitted native command execution. Construction runs no command, so writing the plan still executes nothing. The commands are declared literally by the design and are not executed from the plan. Whether that matches the intended meaning of executeFromPlan is unruled. These questions remain owner rulings (req-pressure:01, req-pressure:02, req-pressure:15):
- whether any per-turn limit survives;
- whether traversal parity is owed;
- whether ordering requires sequential activations.

The current native lifecycle is not evidence that the legacy traversal is reproduced.

